Bernoulli and Euler cache checks
================================

  ./maxima-local --no-init --lisp=sbcl \
    --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=[rtest16]);'

Run every configured Lisp, then the complete core+share suite. The appended
checks retain the existing problem numbers in rtest16. Require the complete
requested count and compare its error summary with the Lisp's baseline;
Maxima can catch Lisp errors and still exit zero.

Each cache has a lock covering lookup, growth and publication. Storage only
grows; a completed prefix never retreats when a smaller producer finishes.
Bernoulli numerator and denominator slots are filled before their limit is
published. Recurrences and nested simplification run outside the lock so a
callback can join another worker. A competing cache miss may duplicate exact
computation. Both zerobern conventions and float conversion remain intact.

The helper computes exact reference coefficients by reciprocal power series:
Bernoulli t/(exp(t)-1) and Euler 1/cosh(t), as defined in NIST DLMF 24.2,
https://dlmf.nist.gov/24.2.E1 and https://dlmf.nist.gov/24.2.E6.
It does not call Maxima's binomial recurrence. Sixty-four generated finite
power sums also check Bernoulli polynomials against direct integer addition.

Tests reset the actual cache arrays in an otherwise idle process, exercise
cold/warm caches and native/fallback scheduling, and restore the original
arrays and limit properties after joining all workers. A temporary PUTPROP
observer reads B36 at publication. Other checks simulate reserved capacity,
completion in decreasing index order, recurrence failure, and nested/native
callbacks through NXTBINCOEF. Intercepted functions are restored on unwinding.
These interceptions are test instrumentation, not application interfaces.

These checks establish this cache contract; other mutable simplifier state
and asynchronous interruption policy require their own verification.
